  "verified_against": {
    "source": "release-manifest.json",
    "agrees": true,
    "note": "Hashes were downloaded from the published v5.45.0 release assets and hashed independently, then checked again by re-fetching the URL this pin itself names. Only the pegasus artifact changed: install.sh and the two builder scripts are byte-identical to v5.28.0 and earlier. v5.45.0 reaches this distribution through the agents it renders under its own name, and it changes them in three ways. First, the three phase-less specialists recover the instruments of their SDD twins. They were extracted to carry the same craft without the SDD chain, but were never added to the reaches lists of the MCP descriptors, so each shipped with strictly less than the twin it was modelled on: the explorer had no shell at all, and none of the three had a browser, a code graph or library documentation. The commit that created them explains why their native tool lists differ and never mentions an MCP server, so this was an omission rather than a decision. It was observed costing real work: a verifier asked to verify a rendered-page fix had to answer that it could not, having nothing to open the page with. They still do not receive write, and that is deliberate -- the SDD twins write their phase document, these return a finding and persist nothing. Second, two agent bodies stopped claiming an enforcement they do not have. The explorer's text said the rendered permissions agreed with its promise not to write; once a shell is granted that is false, because a shell writes and no permission stops it. The verifier had been saying the same thing for longer, with the same shell, unnoticed. Both now name which half is enforced -- edit and write really are denied -- and which half is the agent's word alone. Third, a generated reference now lands beside the craft files: one row per delegation target with the tools and MCP servers an install actually grants it, and a one-line pointer in each agent that delegates to someone other than itself. It is a reference rather than text embedded in every prompt, so it is read when a brief is being written and costs nothing otherwise, which is the same move that took the MCP conventions out of twelve unconditional prompts. It names withheld tools too: a server shown as cbm (withholds: ...) grants everything except those, and an adversarial review caught the first version stating the server bare, which would have invited exactly the wrongly-assumed capability the file exists to close. This distribution changed nothing of its own for this pin beyond the alignment."
  }
